    "produced_at": "2026-07-14",
    "note": "Split-source series (2020-2024): CPCB NWMP annual reports AND KSPCB monthly water-quality reports; where the two differ at a station this file uses CPCB's annual mean (platform-wide convention). Station coordinates are government-record GPS locations, field-verified with Paani Earth against the KSPCB station register (Jul 2026). All three rivers are CPCB Priority I polluted stretches (October 2025 PRS report, mirrored under /docs).",
    "conventions": {
      "aggregation": "readings are annual means; dry-season/monsoon variance is large (see _note)",
      "coordinates": "stations within 5 km of the OSM river polyline are snapped to it (lat_original/lng_original preserved); farther stations carry off_osm_river_polyline + osm_coverage_caveat - see _osm_coverage_note"
    }
  },
  "last_updated": "2026-07-14",
  "data_year_range": "2020-2024",
  "source": "CPCB NWMP annual reports + KSPCB monthly water quality reports (Karnataka State Pollution Control Board). Bengaluru's 3 rivers are among the most-monitored in India because of the Forward Foundation NGT case + the Cauvery-discharge angle. Station coordinates are government-record GPS locations, field-verified with Paani Earth (KSPCB station register, Jul 2026); markers are NOT snapped to OSM river polylines because OSM does not trace these rivers through built-up BBMP. Where CPCB and KSPCB readings differ at the same station, this file uses CPCB's annual mean (consistent with the rest of the platform). All three rivers are CPCB-classified Priority I polluted stretches (the worst category).",
  "_note": "Readings are annual means; the per-month variance is large because the rivers go from low-flow dry-season effluent-dominated to monsoon-diluted within a 3-month window. CPCB's Designated Best-Use thresholds: DO >= 6 mg/L (Class A), BOD <= 2 mg/L (Class A); DO >= 5 / BOD <= 3 (Class B drinking); DO >= 4 / BOD <= 3 (Class C); DO >= 4 / BOD <= 3 + low conductivity (Class D irrigation); >=Class E means unfit for any use. Most Bengaluru river stations fall in Class E.",
